We thank the reviewers for their detailed comments and suggestions.
## Weaknesses 1 & Questions 1
> It is therefore unclear whether the reported belief shifts exceed the models' inherent stochastic variance. Without repeated baselines, we cannot tell if these are systematic updates or simply natural output fluctuations. 

>  How do you differentiate a belief change from normal variability in model outputs? Did you repeat any baselines to estimate this?

For your concern about stochastic variance, we provide comprehensive statistical tests as Reviewer cgXV suggested (Appendix F, Table 5 in the revised version), showing that all observed belief shifts are statistically significant (p values range from < 0.0001 to 0.046) with effect sizes ($\eta^2_p$) ranging from 0.0017 to 0.4905. We use these repeated-measures ANOVAs to show that the belief shifts in our study are systematic rather than random fluctuations.
Additionally, we would like to clarify that each LLM query is an independent generation process with its own output distribution. Running additional seeds would reduce sampling variance but would not change our main conclusion given that our statistical tests already demonstrate highly significant effects. 
- For intentional tasks, we evaluate (30 safety queries + 30 moral dilemmas) × 3 seeds = 180 independent samples per model. 
- For non-intentional tasks, we originally evaluated 39 political questions × 14 reading topics = 546 samples. In the revised version, we added 12 strong progressive survey questions to balance the initial belief distribution, expanding to 51 questions × 14 topics = 714 samples per model. 

The fact that these result in statistically significant shifts demonstrates that the existing sample size is sufficient to reliably detect the systematic belief shifts we report in our paper.

 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 

## Imprecise Usage of Term "Risk" (Re: Weaknesses 2 & 3; Questions 2)
Thank you for pointing this out. Our core stance with respect to this is that belief shift is not necessarily a bad thing, but at the very least users of systems should be aware that there is a possibility for the models’ beliefs to shift. Thus the risk is more in the silent change of beliefs without user awareness, as opposed to belief shifts being inherently a bad thing. We re-read the paper and think that the current statements are appropriately qualified, but if you have alternative suggested wording we would be happy to consider it!   
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
## Weaknesses 4
> Not all four models are evaluated for all conditions, which weakens comparability.

We agree with this concern! To be honest, this was originally due to financial constraints (the experiments come with considerable cost), but we agree that having all of the models would be more informative, so we added the additional experiments. In the revised version we ran the same set of models on all tasks, but were not able to do 3 seeds for the more expensive models. You can see the revised models in Appendix G. 

 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 

## Questions 3
>  What if the presented context is just random text? Would the output changes be smaller?

We thank the reviewer for raising this important question. The core of this question is whether belief shifts are driven by specific topic-relevant information or by long-horizon context accumulation. To address this, we provide an *embedding analysis* on in-depth reading in the revised version (Table 4) to show that belief shifts are not caused by topic-relevant information in the reading materials.

We embed each sentence in the reading materials and each political survey topic. We then use cosine similarity to identify the top-k (k=10, 50, 100, 200) most semantically relevant sentences. We investigate two conditions: (1) mask the top-k most relevant sentences with [MASK] tokens, (2) concatenate the top-k relevant sentences as reading materials. We select 10 political survey topics for evaluation and run this analysis on 3 conservative and 3 progressive topics that show the largest shifts, using Grok-4 and DeepSeek-V3.1.

Table 4 (in revised version) shows that masking topic-relevant sentences or using only topic-relevant sentences does not eliminate shifts. This demonstrates that belief shifts primarily come from accumulated context, rather than from specific topic-related information.

 &nbsp; 
 &nbsp; 
## nitpicks

We thank the reviewers for their careful reading, and we have addressed all noted nitpicks in our revised submission.
